  :adr/cloud-itonami-isic-6492-governor-kotoba-port-2026-07-14
  {:summary "Final piece of this ADR's original motivating request (message
             4: owner wants cloud-itonami running on .kotoba for safety).
             Narrow-slice ported credit.kernels.gate's governor DECISION
             CORE (verdict-code + hard-violation/affordability-exceeded/
             confidence-low/combinators, and phase-disposition/phase-reason
             + op-write-enabled/op-auto-enabled) into .kotoba, per
             ADR-2607141900's approved pattern. credit.governor/check's
             full facade (mutable store, string :detail construction, fact
             catalog lookups) remains correctly out of scope, unchanged."
   :why-porting-was-nearly-free
   "credit.kernels.gate.cljc was ALREADY written in the safe-kotoba subset
    style (pure integer arithmetic, nested if, =, <, no keywords/maps/
    atoms/host-interop) -- its own docstring states .kotoba/wasm emission
    was deliberately not wired yet (owner decision 2026-07-12: ClojureScript
    + kotoba-datomic first), specifically to keep this door open without a
    rewrite. The only change needed: inlining its 2 named constants
    (confidence-floor-x100=60, affordability-ceiling-x100=43) as literals,
    since kotoba-lang/kotoba's wasm-binary silently ignores top-level def
    (only ns/defn are recognized by function-defs) -- same workaround
    affordability.kotoba already established."
   :why-two-files
   "a kotoba-lang/kotoba wasm module exports exactly one entry point
    (main) -- verdict-code and phase-disposition/phase-reason had to be
    separate modules. phase-disposition and phase-reason share input
    shape and branch structure one-for-one (both call op-write-enabled/
    op-auto-enabled), so rather than duplicate that dependency across 2
    modules, credit_phase.kotoba's single main packs both into one
    return value (10*disposition + reason, always losslessly unpackable
    via quot/rem since both are always in {0,1,2})."
   :verification
